      "heading": "LoRA: learn the update, not the weights",
      "narration": "Here is the idea, and it is one line of linear algebra with enormous consequences. When you fine-tune, what you end up with is the original weight matrix plus a change: W plus delta-W. The change is the only thing that is task-specific — W was already there. So the question is not how to store a new model; it is how to store delta-W cheaply. And the observation behind LoRA is that delta-W has low intrinsic rank. Adapting a model to a task does not move the weights in all sixteen million directions available to them; it moves them in a small number of directions, and the rest of the matrix is essentially unchanged. If that is true, then delta-W can be factored: instead of one d-by-d matrix, write it as B times A, where A is r by d and B is d by r, and r is small — eight, or sixteen, or sixty-four. The product is still d by d, so it slots into the model unchanged, but you only ever store and train A and B. Put numbers on it, because the numbers are the argument. For a model dimension of four thousand and ninety-six, the original matrix has about sixteen point seven million entries. With r equal to eight, A and B together have sixty-five thousand. That is about four tenths of one percent of the parameters, trainable, and the other ninety-nine point six percent is frozen. Frozen means no gradient, which means no optimizer state, and remember that optimizer state was three of the four copies in our memory calculation. So the saving is much larger than the parameter count alone suggests: a seven-billion fine-tune goes from something needing eighty gigabytes and multiple accelerators to something that fits comfortably on a single consumer graphics card. Two practical consequences that matter as much as the memory. First, what you save at the end is not a model — it is the adapter, A and B, a file of a few megabytes. The base model is unchanged and shared. So you can hold one copy of a seven-billion-parameter model in memory and serve twenty different specializations from it by swapping which adapter is applied, per request. Ten fine-tunes now cost you fourteen gigabytes plus a few tens of megabytes, instead of a hundred and forty gigabytes. That single property is why LoRA took over. Second, a small design detail that is doing real work: B is initialized to zeros while A is initialized randomly. So at the very start of training, B times A is exactly zero, the adapted model is bit-for-bit the original model, and training begins from a known-good state and departs from it gradually. Without that, you would start by injecting random noise into a carefully pretrained model, which is a bad way to begin. And because the change is additive and separate, it is completely reversible — you delete the adapter and you have the original back, which you cannot say for a full fine-tune.",
