      "heading": "QLoRA, and the settings that actually matter",
      "narration": "LoRA solved the optimizer-state problem, and then QLoRA solved what was left, which was that you still had to hold the frozen base model in memory at full precision. The insight is simple once stated: the base model is never updated. Gradients flow through it but nothing is written to it. So its precision only has to be good enough to compute a forward pass and pass a gradient along — it does not need the precision required to accumulate tiny optimizer updates, because it never accumulates anything. So quantize it to four bits. Keep the adapter in sixteen bits, because that is the part that is actually learning. The result is that fine-tuning a seventy-billion-parameter model fits on a single forty-eight-gigabyte card, which two years earlier would have been an absurd claim. There are a few clever details in the paper — a four-bit format designed for the way neural network weights are actually distributed, quantizing the quantization constants, and paging optimizer state to CPU memory when a spike would otherwise crash the run — but the load-bearing idea is the one sentence: a frozen weight needs less precision than a trained one. Now the settings, because there are only four that matter and people waste a lot of time on them. Rank, r, is the size of the bottleneck and the main dial. Eight to sixteen is enough for almost everything people actually want — adopting a tone, following a specific output format, learning a domain vocabulary. Go to sixty-four or higher only when you are teaching genuinely new behaviour rather than adjusting existing behaviour. And resist the instinct that a bigger rank is a safer default: more capacity mostly buys you a faster route to overfitting on the small dataset you are fine-tuning with. Which matrices to adapt. The original paper applied LoRA only to the query and value projections in attention, and that was mostly a demonstration that a very small intervention suffices. Current practice targets every linear layer, including the feedforward — remember that the feedforward holds about two thirds of the model's weights, so leaving it out means leaving most of the model unreachable. Adapting everything at a low rank generally beats adapting a little at a high rank. Alpha, which confuses everyone, is a scaling factor: the adapter's contribution is multiplied by alpha over r. Its purpose is to let you change r without also changing the effective size of the update. The practical advice is to fix alpha at twice r and then treat r as your only dial. People who tune alpha and r independently are usually tuning the same underlying quantity from two directions and getting confused about which one did what. And the last one, briefly: learning rate. LoRA adapters want a much higher learning rate than full fine-tuning — one e minus four rather than two e minus five is a reasonable starting point — because you are training a small number of freshly initialized parameters rather than nudging a large number of already-good ones. Using a full fine-tuning learning rate is the most common reason a LoRA run appears to do nothing.",
